WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.675
Next door lives a neighbour who cooks the old way.

2
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:11.268
Ask her for a pie, and she hands you a ticket: come back with this, and the pie will be ready.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.101
In code, the neighbour is Java, and her ticket is a CompletableFuture.

4
00:00:16.101 --> 00:00:20.571
A future is an object that stands for a result that isn't ready yet.

5
00:00:20.571 --> 00:00:23.266
Our kitchen runs on bookmarks and timers.

6
00:00:23.266 --> 00:00:24.647
Hers runs on tickets.

7
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:27.705
The lab's bakery library works the same way.

8
00:00:27.705 --> 00:00:34.608
loavesLeft of rye returns a future at once, and later, the future completes with the number of loaves left.

9
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:38.109
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:39.846
A future has a get method.

11
00:00:39.846 --> 00:00:43.322
It waits for the answer, and it works in every demo.

12
00:00:43.322 --> 00:00:44.993
So why not just call get?

13
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.791
Here's how many of us write it: get, inside withContext(Dispatchers.IO).

14
00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:53.043
Our coroutine isn't blocked, it works, so it ships.

15
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.617
Now watch the kitchen.

16
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:57.073
Dispatchers.IO is the bench by the door.

17
00:00:57.073 --> 00:01:04.502
get sends a helper from the bench to the neighbour's fence, and she stands there, holding the ticket, for the whole wait.

18
00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:06.159
She can't do anything else.

19
00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:08.063
She's a doorstop with a ticket.

20
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:12.719
Worse, the guests phone and call dinner off, so our coroutine is cancelled.

21
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.222
But the helper is stuck inside get, and get doesn't listen.

22
00:01:16.222 --> 00:01:20.319
Nobody tells the neighbour, and she bakes a pie that nobody will eat.

23
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:26.343
So get costs twice: a thread held for the whole wait, and a cancel that stops at the fence.

24
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:29.281
What we want is the cook we've had all along.

25
00:01:29.281 --> 00:01:34.286
She leaves a bookmark, goes back to her pots, and returns when the ticket is ready.

26
00:01:34.286 --> 00:01:37.543
And if dinner's off, she passes a note over the fence.

27
00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:38.327
That's await.

28
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:42.379
await suspends until the future completes, so no thread waits.

29
00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:46.410
If the future fails, you catch the real exception, not a wrapper.

30
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.628
And if the waiting coroutine is cancelled, await cancels the future.

31
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:52.277
Let's check.

32
00:01:52.277 --> 00:01:58.116
The lesson's FutureBridge asks the neighbour for a pie that takes 500 milliseconds.

33
00:01:58.116 --> 00:02:03.392
A coroutine awaits it, and after 100 milliseconds, we cancel the coroutine.

34
00:02:03.392 --> 00:02:05.924
Is the neighbour's future cancelled?

35
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:09.081
Yes.

36
00:02:09.081 --> 00:02:11.273
Future cancelled: true.

37
00:02:11.273 --> 00:02:13.179
The note got across.

38
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.026
Sometimes you don't want that: the future is shared, or the pie is already in the oven.

39
00:02:19.026 --> 00:02:21.612
Then asDeferred, then await, only waits.

40
00:02:21.612 --> 00:02:25.751
Same test: future cancelled, false, and the pie still comes out.

41
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:27.749
Now the other way.

42
00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:32.345
The neighbour wants a dish from us, so our kitchen hands her a ticket.

43
00:02:32.345 --> 00:02:34.249
That's the lab's KitchenDoor.

44
00:02:34.249 --> 00:02:43.639
Its cookAsync calls future, a builder like async: it starts a coroutine in the door's own scope, and returns the result as a CompletableFuture.

45
00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:45.810
And this link runs both ways.

46
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.776
If the Java caller cancels that future, or her timeout fires, our coroutine is cancelled.

47
00:02:51.776 --> 00:02:58.479
In FutureBridge, Java cancels the leek soup, and the Kotlin side prints: cooking leek was cancelled.

48
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.699
And because the door owns its scope, closing the door cancels everything still cooking.

49
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.492
Here the picture breaks a little.

50
00:03:06.492 --> 00:03:09.992
Cancelling a plain Java future only marks it as cancelled.

51
00:03:09.992 --> 00:03:15.967
Whether the work stops is up to the code that made it, like a neighbour who may not read your note.

52
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:19.588
A future from our future builder always stops its coroutine.

53
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.466
So, cross the fence with await and future.

54
00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:25.957
get and runBlocking hold a thread, and drop the cancel.

55
00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:34.150
The neighbour across the road cooks with Reactor, a Java library where a Mono is a recipe for at most one value.

56
00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.655
Nothing runs until someone subscribes.

57
00:03:36.655 --> 00:03:39.885
The mono builder turns our suspend code into one.

58
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:50.956
The lesson's ReactorBridge builds a Mono that looks up the soup recipe, then waits for it twice with awaitSingle, which suspends until the Mono's one value arrives.

59
00:03:50.956 --> 00:03:52.081
How many lookups?

60
00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:57.912
Two, and none while the Mono was being built.

61
00:03:57.912 --> 00:04:01.161
mono is cold: one coroutine for each subscriber.

62
00:04:01.161 --> 00:04:02.244
And dragon stew?

63
00:04:02.244 --> 00:04:07.523
The book, sensibly, has nothing, so the Mono is empty, and awaitSingle throws.

64
00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:15.322
Plain Java can't call a suspend function in any useful way: it would have to hand over the hidden bookmark.

65
00:04:15.322 --> 00:04:23.159
So give it doors: a future door like KitchenDoor, a Mono door for Reactor code, or a blocking door with its own timeout.

66
00:04:23.159 --> 00:04:27.404
One suspend function stays the real code, and every door is thin.

67
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:31.153
Which kitchen should a new program start in?

68
00:04:31.153 --> 00:04:34.006
Virtual threads, for mostly blocking calls.

69
00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:39.978
Coroutines, for one deadline over several calls, cancels that reach every child, and Flow.

70
00:04:39.978 --> 00:04:42.434
Reactor, where it's already in place.

71
00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:44.424
And convert only at the edges.

72
00:04:44.424 --> 00:04:52.785
A coroutine that calls runBlocking, which subscribes to a Mono, which blocks on a database, is three kitchens to boil one egg.

73
00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.929
Here's KitchenDoor in Kotlin, and below it, the Java caller's side, which is plain CompletableFuture code.

74
00:05:00.929 --> 00:05:06.115
If her 800 millisecond timeout fires, the coroutine behind it is cancelled.

75
00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:08.510
So, why not just call get?

76
00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.693
Because get stands a thread at the fence, and the cancel stops there.

77
00:05:12.693 --> 00:05:16.089
await waits with a bookmark, and passes the note across.

78
00:05:16.089 --> 00:05:20.272
And future hands the neighbour a ticket that's tied to our coroutine.

79
00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:21.342
Quick check.

80
00:05:21.342 --> 00:05:29.058
A Java caller calls cookAsync, then get, and her thread is interrupted, Java's way of asking a thread to stop.

81
00:05:29.058 --> 00:05:31.443
Is the Kotlin coroutine cancelled?

82
00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:34.639
No.

83
00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:37.043
The interrupt only makes get throw.

84
00:05:37.043 --> 00:05:44.736
The future isn't cancelled, so the coroutine keeps cooking, until someone cancels the future or closes the door.
